 ## Overview
-This method deforms volumetric objects by reducing the complexity of 3D manipulation to a 2D user-guided illustration. The user defines control points on a 2D projection (slice, MIP, or composite) and displaces them; scattered data interpolation using Radial Basis Functions (RBFs) generates a 2D displacement map, which is then extruded into a 3D displacement map and applied to the volume [S1]. It differs from [Incremental Scaling Algorithm](7792-incremental-scaling-algorithm.md) and [Incremental Geometric Transformation](10583-incremental-geometric-transformation.md) by relying on global smooth interpolation from sparse 2D handles rather than local voxel-wise or mesh-element-wise transformations, allowing for complex, plausible deformations with minimal user input [S1].
+This method deforms volumetric objects by reducing the complexity of 3D manipulation to a 2D user-guided illustration [S1]. The user defines control points on a 2D projection (slice, MIP, or composite) and displaces them; scattered data interpolation using Radial Basis Functions (RBFs) generates a 2D displacement map, which is then extruded into a 3D displacement map and applied to the volume [S1]. It differs from [Incremental Scaling Algorithm](7792-incremental-scaling-algorithm.md) and [Incremental Geometric Transformation](10583-incremental-geometric-transformation.md) by relying on global smooth interpolation from sparse 2D handles rather than local voxel-wise or mesh-element-wise transformations, allowing for complex, plausible deformations with minimal user input [S1].
 
